/*
	Stopping a process while asynchronous work on it is finishing

	ExecuteAsync(<process ID>, <script>) runs the script on a worker thread; when the script ends, the worker cleans up
	after it on that same thread, and the cleanup disposes the ServerScript the target process holds. StopProcess
	disposes the process, which walks the same scripts and batches. The two have to be serialized against each other,
	and until ServerProcess.Dispose took the process's call latch they were not: a StopProcess landing in the window
	between the script's last call and the worker's cleanup found an idle process, returned at once, and ran down the
	same object graph beside the worker. The teardown loops are check-then-act over collections the other thread is
	emptying, so one of them dereferenced an emptied slot, and what a D4 author saw was the engine's message for any
	null dereference under a plan node - "A nil was encountered where a value was expected" - reported against the
	StopProcess statement. The statement's argument was never the problem.

	The two 20 ms sleeps are what line the worker's cleanup up with the stop, and 200 iterations are what make a rare
	window certain to be hit. Both are measured, not chosen: on one slow development laptop in Debug on 2026-11-23,
	without the latch this shape failed all five of five consecutive runs, each within 2-3 s, and a probe that counted
	the failures instead of raising saw 15 and 29 of them over 400 iterations, the first at iteration 22 and 65 - about
	one iteration in 20, so 200 leaves a wide margin. With the latch it passed five consecutive runs at about 8 s of
	case time. Do not shrink the loop or the sleeps without remeasuring against an engine whose Dispose does not take
	the latch: a loop too short to hit the window is a test that reports nothing.
*/

begin
	var LIndex := 0;
	var LProcessID := 0;
	while LIndex < 200 do
	begin
		LProcessID := StartProcess();
		try
			ExecuteAsync(LProcessID, 'Sleep(20);');
			// Long enough that the asynchronous script is finishing, and its worker about to clean up after it, when
			// the StopProcess below runs.
			Sleep(20);
		finally
			StopProcess(LProcessID);
		end;
		LIndex := LIndex + 1;
	end;
end;
